---
// Lesson 7.2a — Tile Collision, Layers & Sorting
// TilemapCollider2D + CompositeCollider2D; layers and sorting layers.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>7.2a — Tile Collision, Layers &amp; Sorting</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Painted tiles are pretty pictures until physics says they are
    rock. Today: what makes a tilemap solid, why every tilemap ships
    with a partner called Composite, and the two different meanings
    of "layer" that confuse every beginner exactly once.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — the collider that is 500 colliders.</strong>
    Select the ground Tilemap child:
    <code>Add Component → Tilemap Collider 2D</code>. Press Play:
    you can stand on your cave. What just happened: the component
    builds <strong>one small collider per solid tile</strong>. A
    40-tile floor is 40 colliders. It works — and it is quietly
    expensive.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — the composite.</strong> Now add
    <code>Add Component → Composite Collider 2D</code>. Unity
    attaches a Rigidbody 2D automatically (the tilemap's own, set to
    static — let it). Back on the Tilemap Collider 2D, check
    <strong>Used By Composite</strong>. Press Play again: same solid
    floor, but the 40 colliders are fused into ONE shape. Why it
    matters to you beyond performance: your ground raycast from
    <a href="6.2a.html">6.2a</a> fires one question per frame — a
    composite answers once, cleanly. And your character no longer
    catches on the seams between tiles (the invisible-wall bug).</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — the two "layers," untangled.</strong></p>
    <table>
        <tr><th></th><th>Layer (physics)</th><th>Sorting Layer (render)</th></tr>
        <tr><td>What it controls</td><td>Who collides with whom; raycasts; masks</td><td>What draws in front of what</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lives on</td><td>Inspector top-right</td><td>SpriteRenderer → Sorting Layer</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Game use</td><td>Ground / Player / Enemies / Projectiles</td><td>Background / Ground / Player / FX / UI</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p>
        Do both now: create physics layers Ground, Player, Enemy,
        Projectile, Decoration and assign them; create sorting layers
        Background, Ground, Entities, FX and assign those. When your
        player draws BEHIND a wall, that is sorting; when your raycast
        hits a decoration, that is physics layers. Different walls,
        different fixes.
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The invisible wall</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Walk along your tile floor and randomly stop mid-tile.
                The seam bug! Which component in Step 2 makes seams
                disappear — and why did physics see a wall that art
                could not?</li>
            <li>Paint decorations (background rocks) on the GROUND
                tilemap. Now your ground raycast hits a decorative
                pebble. Which of the two layer systems fixes it —
                and how (a separate tilemap + its own physics
                layer)?</li>
            <li>Set the enemy prefab to the Ground physics layer by
                accident. What did you just make the ENEMY stand on
                — and why does your player fall THROUGH the same
                enemy?</li>
            <li>The turret's projectile raycast-ignores the turret
                (<a href="3.2a.html">3.2a</a>). Which fix would
                layers give you — and which is already in the
                starter (spawn OUTSIDE the turret's own collider)?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Design Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Depth is a sorting-layer performance. Build this vertical
        slice in your cave, using at least three sorting layers:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>Background: dark distant rocks (no physics layer, no
            collider — pure picture)</li>
        <li>Ground: the playable rock</li>
        <li>Entities: player, enemy, turret</li>
        <li>FX: your coin sparkle / attack swing (draws over
            entities — and OVER the player, which is why it must be
            its own layer)</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The AP exam asks how computing can trade resource use
            against performance — 500 tile colliders vs. one
            composite is a case study. What resource did you spend
            (a component, a moment of setup) and what did you save
            (per-frame checks)? Frame it in one sentence like a
            Create PT response.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Depth tricks</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Make a FOREGROUND pillar (sorting layer above the
                player) the player walks BEHIND. What classic game
                moment did you just build (hiding!)?</li>
            <li>One-way platforms: the tile you can jump UP through
                but stand ON. What property of the collider (not a
                layer!) does this need — hint: Platform Effector
                2D. Try it on one tile group.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Make a tilemap solid: Tilemap Collider + Composite + Used By Composite</li>
            <li>Explain the seam bug and what the composite fixes</li>
            <li>Keep physics layers and sorting layers straight, with one use for each</li>
            <li>Build a 3+ sorting-layer scene with a foreground hide</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-07/index.html">Unit 7: Level Building &amp; Ladders</a> — colliders and layers</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — the component cheat-sheet</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="7.3a.html">7.3a — Ladders I: The Climbing State</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="7.2a" title="7.2a Tile Collision, Layers &amp; Sorting — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
